VIlkan — NOTICE / CREDITS
=========================

VIlkan is built on VIctory, an Xbox Series X|S → Windows compatibility layer
(the title's x86-64 code runs natively; the platform is supplied underneath it).
We add our own Linux runtime and wave64 work, and a native Vulkan translation
path. This file names what is upstream, what we changed, and what is ours.

Upstream: VIctory
-----------------
The library crates are upstream VIctory, kept under their original names so we
can keep pulling upstream updates:

    crates/victory-core      config, asset-policy guard, logging
    crates/victory-pe        PE32+ / import-library analysis
    crates/victory-loader    prepare, map, bind, start the title image
    crates/victory-hle       console API implementations (HLE)
    crates/victory-gpu       D3D12.X objects / command lists → host D3D12
    crates/victory-shader    Xbox shader containers → host shaders
    crates/victory-formats   texture layouts, audio codecs, BCPack
    crates/victory-pipeline  converter trait, content-addressed cache
    crates/victory-game      title-specific formats / build identities

Upstream configuration and marker files keep their names: `victory.toml`,
`victory.example.toml`, `.victory-root`. The upstream environment variables
(`VICTORY_GAME_DIR`, `VICTORY_CACHE_DIR`, `VICTORY_DATA_DIR`, `VICTORY_LOG`,
`VICTORY_CONFIG`, …) are read by the upstream-derived code unchanged.

VIctory is GPL-3.0-or-later (see LICENSE). VIlkan is GPL-3.0-or-later; derived
portions are GPL-compatible under it.

What we changed in upstream-derived files
-----------------------------------------
Functional changes (kept; identifiers reverted to upstream names):

  * victory-shader: a backend-neutral wave64→wave32 exchange
    (`emit/exchange.rs`, ours) wired into `emit/lower.rs` and `emit/mod.rs`,
    with a host wave-width gate. This is the DXIL/Proton path's wave64 fix.
  * victory-gpu: a host wave-width query (`host.rs`) and a refused-shader dump
    (`pipeline/host.rs`); our window title/class and adapter strings (VIlkan).
  * victory-formats: BCPack tables loaded from outside the tree
    (`VILKAN_BCPACK_TABLES`), since the BC6H/BC7 history is game-derived.
  * xtask: the asset policy runs with zero exceptions; `.py` is allowlisted.

Everything else in the upstream-derived crates is upstream code, restored
byte-identical from our fork base, with only the game-name scrub applied
(see scripts/scrub.py).

What is ours (branded VIlkan)
-----------------------------
  * apps/vilkan            the command-line front end / binary (vilkan.exe)
  * crates/vilkan-vk       Linux-native Vulkan runtime (ash): device, pipelines,
                           dispatch of sidecar-compiled SPIR-V — no Proton/D3D12
  * crates/victory-shader/src/emit/exchange.rs (+ tests)  the wave64 exchange
  * crates/victory-shader/examples/extract-native.rs      native-code extractor
  * scripts/               build.sh, run.sh, compile-native.py, scrub.py
  * docs/                  linux-proton.md, native-vulkan.md, wave64-exchange.md,
                           architecture.md, asset-policy.md
  * our environment variables: VILKAN_DATA, VILKAN_GAME_DIR, VILKAN_TITLE_EXE,
    VILKAN_SHADER_FIXTURES, VILKAN_BCPACK_TABLES, VILKAN_VK_FIXTURES, VILKAN_LOG

Vendored: RDNA2 → SPIR-V translator (third_party/rdna2spirv)
-----------------------------------------------------------
The native Vulkan path consumes SPIR-V compiled by a clean-room RDNA2 → SPIR-V
translator, vendored under `third_party/rdna2spirv/` so the native path works
from a clean clone with no external dependency. We invoke it as a compiler; we
do not port it to Rust or write our own IR→SPIR-V emitter.

It is **GPL-3.0** software (its license is `third_party/rdna2spirv/LICENSE`);
VIlkan is GPL-3.0-or-later, so the combination is GPL-compatible. Vendored
components: the translator (`rdna2spirv.py`), the disassembler (`shader_disasm.py`,
`rdna2_decode.py`), the shader IR (`shader_ir.py`), the wave64 emulation modules
(`wave64_emulate_opt.py`, `wave64_halves.py`, `lane_masks.py`, `cfg_structure.py`),
the host subgroup probe (`vk_subgroup_info.py`), the fixture build harness
(`runtime/test/shaders/build.py`, `compute_fixture.py`) and their module
dependencies. The synthetic cross-half fixtures (`cs_w64x_*.asm`) are clean-room
test code. `tools/gfx10_formats.json` is the public GFX10 format enum from Mesa
(`src/amd/registers/gfx10-rsrc.json`), a hardware format table, not game data.

Every vendored file was audited before import: clean-room code only; anything
game-derived, fitted or calibrated was rejected (the BCPack BC6H/BC7 history
dictionaries are the precedent — those stay outside the tree). The game-name
scrub was run over the vendored files; no title-identifying string is tracked.

Game-name scrub and asset policy
--------------------------------
The supported title is referred to only as "Chores Simulator Miami" (CSM). The
real game name, publisher, release/scene tags and title-identifying strings are
stripped by scripts/scrub.py (idempotent, re-run after every upstream sync).
Nothing game-derived is tracked; the asset policy runs with zero exceptions.
See docs/asset-policy.md and docs/sync.md.